      "content": "I have corrected the spelling, grammar, and punctuation throughout the file.\n\n<smtcmp_block filename=\"writing/the root.md\" language=\"markdown\">\nHow can we restructure organisations to be more human and Earth-centric?\nThis is a question that's been with me for the past two decades...\n<!-- ... existing content ... -->\nOver the last few years, I've come to the understanding that the state of the world we are in is the product of the solutions found for the two oldest problems of our species: the coordination problem and allocation problems.\n\n#### coordination:\nHow do we get a group of people to produce drinks? You need people to develop products, to wash bottles, to make the juice, to bottle, to deliver, to talk to clients and so on. As long as it's being done by one person it's easy... with two people you need to divide roles and trust the other parties. This works until about seven people where you divide the work and have meetings to discuss what needs to be done when, what hasn't been done, why, and to resolve issues. Usually by the second to fifth person there is already a hierarchy formed. Why is that? Usually whoever was there before and thus knows more, and whoever has put in the financing to start the project. This hierarchy gets built out and expanded upon. We can say that this hierarchy is unjust, unfair or wrong, but that fails to ask the question: why is it there?\n\nThe hierarchy is there to filter information and to enable collaboration. It's almost impossible for a group to agree on what to do, which means that people take charge...\n<!-- ... existing content ... -->\nA very good founder can manage 20 people under him; above that they will not have time. Usually by 14 people we start to see meetings, committees, and leaders out of necessity to communicate information across the organization. The founder often becomes the bridge across departments. Now as we are trying to set up a non-hierarchical organisation we will create groups. The aspiration is that the people in these groups will take the decisions and then execute. This works until around 200 people; at this point groups start to create subgroups. This is a hierarchy but instead of an individual hierarchy it's a group hierarchy. The hope is that these groups will be democratic; this is rarely the case as people do not like to be liable and to take decisions. These groups will usually have one person taking the decisions and the others voting with them. I have rarely seen a group that was democratic and not just agreeing to one person. The other issue with groups is that if there are players there for a while and they control who comes in in any form, they'll form a group that's subservient to them. And thus our group-based non-hierarchical organisation has collapsed back into a standard hierarchical system.\n\nThe best solution we have found for these problems is hierarchy for coordination and money and markets for resource allocation. In Auroville, a project that tried to go beyond them, I've watched the evolution and studied the origins of these two systems...\n<!-- ... existing content ... -->\nWith the advent of AI I see an opportunity to use it to allocate resources and move information across organizations in a way that we could have only dreamed of five years ago.\n<!-- ... existing content ... -->\n</smtcmp_block>",
